Article 5

(1) Every Woman victim of Violence, Exploitation and Discrimination has:

a. the right to handling;
b. the right of Victims to Protection; and
c. the right of Victims to recovery.
(2) The right to handling as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a includes:
a. the right to information regarding the entire process and results of Handling, Protection, and Recovery; b. the right to obtain handling result documents; c. the right to legal services;
d. the right to psychological reinforcement;
e. the right to health services including examination, treatment, and medical care; f. the right to services and facilities in accordance with the special needs of victims; and
g. the right to deletion of sexual content for cases of sexual violence using electronic media.
(3) The right of Victims to Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b includes:
a. provision of information regarding rights and Protection facilities;
b. provision of access to information on the implementation of Protection;
c. protection from threats or violence from perpetrators and other parties as well as recurrence of violence;
d. protection of identity confidentiality;
e. protection from attitudes and behavior of law enforcement officers that demean Victims;
f. protection from loss of employment, transfer of employment, Education, or political access; and g. protection of Victims and/or reporters from criminal prosecution or civil lawsuits for Criminal Acts of Sexual Violence that have been reported. (4) The right of Victims to recovery as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c includes:
a. medical rehabilitation;
b. mental and social rehabilitation;
c. social empowerment;
d. restitution and/or compensation;
e. social reintegration; and
f. obtaining skills training for economic improvement for vulnerable women.

Article 30

(1) Every person may participate in the organization of the Protection of Women in the Region. (2) Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, groups or community social organizations, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, Law Enforcement Institutions, service institutions, religious institutions, Islamic boarding schools, professional organizations, legal aid organizations, youth organizations, broadcasting supervisory institutions, media, communities, professional institutions, business sector and other related stakeholders.

(3) Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may include:

a. providing information or reporting to the competent authority if aware of acts of violence, and or exploitation and or discrimination against women;
b. conducting preventive actions against violence against women;
c. conducting professional skills training in the form of:
1. leadership training;
2. management training;
3. business communication training;
4. negotiation skills training;
5. technology and digital training; and
6. entrepreneurship training;
d. conducting specific skills training in the form of:
1. self-defense training;
2. creative arts skills training; and
3. handicrafts, cooking skills, sewing skills, child care skills, and household management skills;
e. conducting health and welfare training in the form of:
1. fitness training; and
2. mental health training;
f. conducting community and social training in the form of:
1. activism and advocacy training; and
2. volunteer training in various social, health, and environmental fields;
g. establishing community social institutions that manage the guidance and empowerment of women.
(4) Further provisions regarding community participation as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated in the Mayor Regulation

Article 4

(1) Every woman has the right:

a. to life;
b. to have a family and continue offspring; c. to self-development;
d. to obtain justice;
e. to personal freedom;
f. to security;
g. to welfare; and
h. to participate in government.
(2) The right to life as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a includes:
a. the right to sustain life and improve the standard of living;
b. the right to obtain peace of mind, sense of security, sense of peace, happiness, physical and spiritual welfare; and
c. the right to a good and healthy environment. (3) The right to have a family and continue offspring as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, namely the right to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage in accordance with religion and the provisions of laws and regulations. (4) The right to self-development as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, includes:
a. the right to fulfillment of basic needs to grow and develop properly;
b. the right to protection for personal development, to obtain education, enlighten oneself and improve quality of life in order to become a human being who is faithful, pious, responsible, of noble character, happy, and prosperous in accordance with human rights;
c. the right to develop and obtain benefits from science and technology, arts and culture in accordance with human dignity for the welfare of oneself, nation, and humanity;
d. the right to communicate and obtain information necessary to develop oneself and social environment in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
e. the right to seek, obtain, own, store, process, and convey information using all types of available means in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
f. the right to fight for the right to self-development, both individually and collectively, to build society, nation, and country in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
g. the right to perform social work in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
h. the right to participate in the planning and implementation of government policies in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
i. the right to organize and form organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and
j. the right to organize education and teaching in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms.
(5) The right to obtain justice as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, includes:
a. the right to equal treatment without discrimination;
b. the right to obtain justice;
c. the right to submit applications, complaints, and lawsuits, whether in criminal, civil, or administrative cases; and
d. the right to be tried through a free and impartial judicial process, in accordance with procedural law that guarantees objective examination by honest and fair judges to obtain a just and true decision.
(6) The right to personal freedom as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e, includes:
a. the right not to be enslaved or subjugated, trafficked, and all acts with similar purposes;
b. the right to personal integrity, both spiritually and physically;
c. the right not to become a research object without consent; and
d. the right to freely express and deliver opinions with regard to religious values, morality, order, public interest, and national integrity.
(7) The right to security as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, includes:
a. the right to protection of self, family, honor, dignity, and property;
b. the right to recognition before the law as a human person; and
c. the right to be free from threats to do or not do something that violates religious, social, and legal norms.
(8) The right to welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) letter g, includes:
a. the right to have welfare, either alone or together; and
b. the right to perform work and receive fair wages in accordance with work that is comparable and commensurate with human dignity in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(9) The right to participate in government as referred to in paragraph (1) letter h, includes:
a. the right to be elected and to vote in general elections; regional head elections, and/or other political office elections based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret, honest and fair voting according to applicable laws and regulations;
b. the right to have a career in government; and c. the right to convey aspirations directly or through intermediaries in government.

