b. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women's rights;
c. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system for the prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination;
d. providing easy access to obtain civil rights, education, health, employment, training and funding, welfare improvement and services;
e. developing communication media, information, education and public campaigns through media; f. developing educational materials and curriculum;
g. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
h. building early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential environments, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
i. providing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
j. encouraging behavioral change in parents and society;
k. developing policies related to districts/cities, sub-districts, schools, Islamic boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
l. forming and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, and religious leaders for prevention;
m. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media; n. developing special protection and support systems for vulnerable women groups;
o. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
p. enhancing the capacity of Regional Apparatus, government agencies, educational institutions, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business sectors, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, media, religious leaders, community leaders and communities; and
q. forming cooperation networks in efforts to prevent acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination with law enforcement apparatus, government agencies, Regional Apparatus, community institutions, educational institutions, community leaders, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business sectors, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, communities, and media.
(5) Integrated prevention efforts of Violence, Exploitation and Discrimination against women as referred to in paragraph (1) are coordinated by the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection as well as governmental affairs in the field of population control and family planning.
(6) Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a may involve Regional Apparatus, agencies, community institutions, educational institutions, community leaders, integrated service posts, family learning centers, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business sectors, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, communities, and media.

Article 3

(1) The implementation of protection of women is intended to realize the protection of women from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination.

(2) The implementation of protection of women aims to:

a. prevent all forms of violence, exploitation, and discrimination against women;
b. provide services and recovery for women victims;
c. fulfillment of the special needs of women; and d. improve the quality of life of women. (3) The scope of regulation of the implementation of protection of women includes:
a. rights of women;
b. efforts to protect women;
c. obligations and responsibilities of Regional Government; d. institutions.
e. coordination and cooperation;
f. participation;
g. monitoring, evaluation and reporting;
h. awards; and
i. financing.

Article 7

The implementation of the Protection of Women includes efforts to:

a. prevent and protect women from acts of violence, exploitation, and discrimination in a fast, integrated, and coordinated manner;
b. provide services to victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination;
c. empower women;
d. fulfill the special needs of women; and e. improve family quality.

Article 6

(1) The forms of Violence referred to in Article 5 include:

a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic violence;
e. exploitation; and
f. KBGO.
(2) Economic Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d includes:
a. taking full control over the victim's income, including salary, savings, or other financial assistance received by the victim:
b. restricting or prohibiting the victim from having or using money, so that the victim is completely dependent on the perpetrator for financial needs:
c. prohibiting or preventing the victim from working or continuing education with the purpose of limiting the victim's financial independence:
d. using the victim's assets or property for personal gain or damaging such assets as a form of punishment; and
e. making all financial decisions without involving the victim, so that the victim has no control over household finances.
(3) Exploitation as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e at a minimum:
a. exploiting women's labor by providing inadequate wages, forcing excessive working hours, or not providing safe and healthy working conditions;
b. exploiting women for sexual purposes, through coercion, deception, or pressure. Including human trafficking for prostitution or pornography production; and
c. exploiting women unfairly in economic transactions, such as providing loans with very high interest or manipulating the market for personal gain.
(4) KBGO as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, includes:
a. comments or messages that are insulting, degrading, or belittling women;
b. messages or comments that threaten to commit physical or sexual violence against women;
c. disseminating the victim's personal information (such as address, phone number, or other identity) without permission with the purpose of intimidating or harming the victim;
d. extortion involving threats to spread intimate images or videos of the victim unless the victim meets the perpetrator's demands;
e. spreading private photos or videos of an intimate nature without the consent of the women in the content;
f. the use of technology to stalk or continuously monitor the victim, often with the purpose of frightening or controlling the victim; and
g. digitally altering images or videos of women for the purpose of insulting, humiliating, or sexually exploiting.

